    "sourceNote": "German and Bavarian dynastic reference scholarship; Heidelberg medieval studies on Conrad IV and Elisabeth, including evidence that no coronation ceremony is attested; Stams Abbey historical materials; Wikipedia used as a supplementary chronology index.",
    "notes": "Elisabeth linked the Wittelsbachs to the last legitimate Hohenstaufen kings through Conrad IV and their son Conradin. After Conrad's death she remarried into the Gorizia-Tyrol house and became an important ancestress of the later Tyrolean line.\n\nConfidence / caveats: Although Elisabeth is conventionally styled Queen of Germany through her marriage to Conrad IV, no coronation ceremony for her is securely attested; title and coronation must not be conflated. Her second marriage is dated 1258 or 1259 in different works. She died before Meinhard II became Duke of Carinthia in 1286, so she must not be titled Duchess of Carinthia.\n\nUnlinked family (not in roster): mother: Agnes of the Palatinate; spouse: Meinhard II, Count of Gorizia-Tyrol; child: Elisabeth of Carinthia, Queen of Germany; child: Otto III, Duke of Carinthia; child: Henry of Carinthia, King of Bohemia",
